WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:01.125
Module four:

2
00:00:01.125 --> 00:00:02.676
decentralized exchanges.

3
00:00:02.676 --> 00:00:07.780
How to swap with a pool instead of an order book, how a formula sets the price,

4
00:00:07.780 --> 00:00:13.142
where providers' impermanent loss comes from, and how bots make money on your swap.

5
00:00:14.142 --> 00:00:17.579
A decentralized exchange is a set of smart contracts.

6
00:00:17.579 --> 00:00:22.701
Keeping an order book on a blockchain is expensive, so instead there is a pool:

7
00:00:22.701 --> 00:00:25.360
a contract holding a stock of two tokens.

8
00:00:25.360 --> 00:00:29.121
Providers deposit both tokens, traders swap with the pool,

9
00:00:29.121 --> 00:00:31.390
and a formula calculates the price.

10
00:00:31.390 --> 00:00:36.318
You connect a wallet and swap tokens straight from it, without registration.

11
00:00:37.318 --> 00:00:39.983
The best-known formula is x·y = k:

12
00:00:39.983 --> 00:00:45.077
the product of the pool's reserves does not change during a swap.

13
00:00:45.077 --> 00:00:50.721
Say the pool holds 100 ether and 200,000 dollars, so the price is 2,000.

14
00:00:50.721 --> 00:00:56.207
To take 10 ether you will have to pay about 22,200, or 2,222 per coin.

15
00:00:56.207 --> 00:00:59.734
The more you take, the dearer each next coin.

16
00:01:00.734 --> 00:01:05.015
Price impact depends on the size of the swap relative to the pool.

17
00:01:05.015 --> 00:01:10.010
In a pool worth hundreds of millions of dollars a 1,000-dollar swap is barely

18
00:01:10.010 --> 00:01:14.356
noticeable; in a 50,000-dollar pool it moves the price by percents.

19
00:01:14.356 --> 00:01:19.350
After a large swap, arbitrageurs pull the pool price back to the market, and,

20
00:01:19.350 --> 00:01:22.334
as we will see now, at the providers' expense.

21
00:01:23.334 --> 00:01:26.607
A provider deposited one ether and 2,000 dollars.

22
00:01:26.607 --> 00:01:32.017
The ether price doubled, and arbitrageurs bought the cheap ether out of the pool.

23
00:01:32.017 --> 00:01:37.360
Now the provider has 5,657 dollars, while simply holding would have given 6,000.

24
00:01:37.360 --> 00:01:41.969
The pool keeps selling what gets dearer and buying what gets cheaper.

25
00:01:41.969 --> 00:01:46.110
The loss is the same for a rise and a fall by the same factor.

26
00:01:47.110 --> 00:01:49.539
A provider earns fees from every swap,

27
00:01:49.539 --> 00:01:52.480
and the result is fees minus impermanent loss.

28
00:01:52.480 --> 00:01:55.356
For two dollar stablecoins the loss is small.

29
00:01:55.356 --> 00:01:59.576
If one coin can crash against the other, the loss eats everything.

30
00:01:59.576 --> 00:02:04.562
And liquidity in a narrow range earns more fees, but when the price leaves it,

31
00:02:04.562 --> 00:02:07.822
you are left entirely in the coin that got cheaper.

32
00:02:08.822 --> 00:02:13.558
A sent transaction first lands in a public queue and is visible to everyone.

33
00:02:13.558 --> 00:02:18.544
A bot sees your large swap, places its own buy before it and a sell right after.

34
00:02:18.544 --> 00:02:21.971
It takes almost all the slippage tolerance you allowed:

35
00:02:21.971 --> 00:02:24.651
if you agreed to receive five percent less,

36
00:02:24.651 --> 00:02:27.206
the bot will try to take almost all five.

37
00:02:28.206 --> 00:02:29.772
How to protect yourself.

38
00:02:29.772 --> 00:02:30.946
A small tolerance:

39
00:02:30.946 --> 00:02:34.274
for liquid pairs, from one tenth to half a percent.

40
00:02:34.274 --> 00:02:36.884
Better to repeat a swap than to overpay.

41
00:02:36.884 --> 00:02:40.080
A large swap — in parts or through an aggregator.

42
00:02:40.080 --> 00:02:41.581
And private submission:

43
00:02:41.581 --> 00:02:45.822
many wallets can pass the transaction straight to block builders.

44
00:02:46.822 --> 00:02:52.128
An aggregator splits a swap across several pools so that each part moves the price

45
00:02:52.128 --> 00:02:52.452
less.

46
00:02:52.452 --> 00:02:56.594
The full cost of a swap is made up of network gas, the pool fee,

47
00:02:56.594 --> 00:02:57.953
price impact and MEV.

48
00:02:57.953 --> 00:03:00.412
What you should compare is the result:

49
00:03:00.412 --> 00:03:02.806
how many tokens you actually receive.

50
00:03:03.806 --> 00:03:04.818
The main points.

51
00:03:04.818 --> 00:03:07.918
On a decentralized exchange you swap with a pool,

52
00:03:07.918 --> 00:03:12.092
and the bigger the swap relative to the pool, the worse the price.

53
00:03:12.092 --> 00:03:15.255
Providers earn fees but pay with impermanent loss.

54
00:03:15.255 --> 00:03:19.430
And a large swap with a high slippage tolerance is a gift to bots.
